WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Across NASA, teams are testing the technologies and systems that will shape the future of

00:05.000 --> 00:07.000
exploration and aviation.

00:07.000 --> 00:10.000
Here's what's new this week in Your NASA Minute.

00:10.000 --> 00:14.000
This week, NASA supported the Department of War and other federal agencies in releasing

00:14.000 --> 00:16.000
files on unidentified anomalous phenomena.

00:16.000 --> 00:20.000
NASA plots President Trump's direction for a whole of government transparency and will

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
always make available our materials and expertise to the investigators at the all-domain

00:25.000 --> 00:29.000
Anomaly Resolution Office, which leads the U.S. government's efforts to address UAP.

00:30.000 --> 00:34.000
NASA took its first steps towards rebuilding the agency's core technical capabilities,

00:34.000 --> 00:39.000
welcoming 20 engineers and technicians into civil service roles under Administrator

00:39.000 --> 00:41.000
Isaac Mint's workforce directive.

00:41.000 --> 00:45.000
By bringing critical expertise in-house, NASA is strengthening the technical foundation

00:45.000 --> 00:49.000
needed for the next era of exploration.

00:49.000 --> 00:54.000
NASA's X-59 quiet supersonic jet is ramping up the pace of flight testing.

00:54.000 --> 00:58.000
At Armstrong Flight Research Center, the experimental aircraft recently completed two

00:58.000 --> 01:04.000
flights in one day, for the first time continuing to fly higher, faster, and through more advanced

01:04.000 --> 01:08.000
maneuvers as NASA works toward quiet supersonic flight.

01:08.000 --> 01:13.000
NASA and Blue Origin are taking another step toward sending scientific and technology payloads

01:13.000 --> 01:15.000
to the lunar service.

01:15.000 --> 01:19.000
At Johnson Space Center, vacuum chamber testing is helping prepare the Blue Moon Mark I cargo

01:19.000 --> 01:23.000
lander for future mission to the moon, which will carry NASA instruments to study lunar

01:23.000 --> 01:27.000
landings and improve navigation for future missions to the moon.

01:28.000 --> 01:33.000
And finally, this week, a small NASA-supported spacecraft carrying a powerful new science instrument

01:33.000 --> 01:37.000
launched into orbit onboard a SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket.

01:37.000 --> 01:42.000
The CubeSat mission, known as Snappy, will test a miniaturized neutrino detector that could one day

01:42.000 --> 01:49.000
help scientists better understand fusion inside the Sun and improve space weather forecasting here on Earth.

01:49.000 --> 01:55.000
Each test, each flight, and each mission brings us closer to turning the near impossible into a reality.

01:55.000 --> 01:57.000
We'll see you next time on The NASA Minute.

01:58.000 --> 02:01.000
Beeping

